Analytic chamfers and roundings cut over a gap by a long chain of faces
beyond, and their pieces about a closed spine's start (mitcad#235).

The cases: the rim of a flange under a ring of teeth (a pulley's, a
pinion's), chamfered on the flange's top as far as, or a little past, the
roots of the teeth: the top is narrower than the chamfer under every
tooth, its contact runs under the tooth, and the tooth's faces cut the
chamfer's cone there and grow down to it (0055, 0071, 0142). Each tooth
has seven faces round it (roundings at its root and tip, flanks, tip).

- ChFi3d_KPartCutChain: the chain of faces beyond from the face the
  contact leaves its face into to the one it comes back from may be nine
  faces long (four before).
- ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve: where no arc of the intersection between the cut's
  ends lies on the face beyond (a face that grows up to the cut, beyond its
  bounds), the arc whose coordinate along the spine spans the least: the
  other arc of a closed intersection (a root rounding's cylinder with the
  cone) runs round the far side of the face's surface, and the piece's cut
  was taken there (KeepCutInPiece then rejected it). And where the cut's
  ends lie on two lines of the intersection that meet end to end, or
  through a third (the intersection split where the surfaces touch: a
  root rounding tangent to the root circle the contact runs along), the
  arc joined along them.
- ChFi3d_FillKPartGaps: for chamfers, a gap whose ends are vertices of the
  contact's face N (the contact runs along N's edges, the root arcs, and
  leaves N where they end): the gap's ends are on N's edges into the faces
  beyond there (ChFi3d_OtherEdgeOfFaceAtVertex, the edge it leaves N
  across, found by where the contact runs before the vertex), and those
  edges, wholly under the chamfer, bound no face any more (cut at their
  vertices with no side kept). Before, such gaps were not filled and the
  chamfer failed ("Chaining is impossible").
- ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges: on a closed spine, the neighbour of the
  first SurfData across the spine's start is the last one, and the other
  way round: a contact along part of an edge that the spine's start splits
  (a root arc across the circle's vertex) runs on along that edge into the
  neighbour, and the edge is not cut there. Before, the edge was cut at
  the spine's start and one part kept beside the blend (an open wire).
- SplitKPart: a piece of one edge of a closed spine of several edges
  whose line runs across its period's end, from before the start of the
  edge into it (a domain of the contact's hatching across the circle's
  origin), is not taken for one wholly past the edge's end: its start a
  period before is. Before, the piece at the edge's start was dropped.

Otherwise as before: a cut with an arc on the face beyond keeps that arc,
one with a single arc across the blend that arc; gaps between arcs, and
pieces of spines of one edge, are made as they were.

Upstream: new.
